WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.679
A robot reads its next moves off a video it imagined.

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:05.703
What if that video only looks right?

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:10.317
Hi, I'm Nico!

00:00:10.317 --> 00:00:14.427
GEM-4D is a video world model for robot arms that learns 3D

00:00:14.427 --> 00:00:17.852
geometry in training, then sheds the part that taught it.

00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:20.060
Chapter one.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.497
Video in, actions out.

00:00:26.692 --> 00:00:28.923
The recipe: don't learn actions directly.

00:00:28.923 --> 00:00:31.154
Imagine the future, then measure it.

00:00:31.574 --> 00:00:35.331
A world model takes the first frame and an instruction, and generates

00:00:35.331 --> 00:00:36.584
the next few seconds.

00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:40.751
An extractor finds the gripper in each frame and lifts it into

00:00:40.751 --> 00:00:42.000
3D with estimated depth.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.433
Inverse kinematics turns that path into joint commands.

00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:48.180
Here is the trap.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.447
On the left, five generated frames: the gripper steps right by

00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:52.635
the same few pixels.

00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:55.011
On the right, what they mean in 3D.

00:00:55.431 --> 00:00:57.517
Make the scene deeper, and the reach grows.

00:00:57.517 --> 00:00:58.821
The frames do not change.

00:00:59.241 --> 00:01:02.023
Or the camera panned, and the gripper barely moved.

00:01:02.023 --> 00:01:02.951
Same frames again.

00:01:03.371 --> 00:01:04.633
Now the real failure.

00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:06.526
Each frame implies its own depth.

00:01:06.526 --> 00:01:09.997
Every frame looks fine, but in 3D the reach zigzags, and

00:01:09.997 --> 00:01:11.259
the arm would follow.

00:01:11.679 --> 00:01:14.082
A pixel loss cannot tell these apart.

00:01:14.082 --> 00:01:16.484
A model with one consistent geometry can.

00:01:18.192 --> 00:01:19.505
The paper writes it down.

00:01:19.505 --> 00:01:22.655
Where a pixel lands in the next frame depends on four things.

00:01:23.075 --> 00:01:24.330
The depth at that pixel.

00:01:25.732 --> 00:01:27.500
How the camera rotated and moved.

00:01:28.779 --> 00:01:31.132
And how the point itself moved, its scene flow.

00:01:31.631 --> 00:01:35.571
Get those four right inside the model, and every correspondence is fixed.

00:01:37.342 --> 00:01:37.921
Chapter two.

00:01:37.921 --> 00:01:39.367
A branch it will lose.

00:01:45.142 --> 00:01:46.094
Here's the idea.

00:01:46.094 --> 00:01:49.901
Bolt a small network onto a middle block of the video model,

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:53.074
and make it predict what a frozen depth model sees.

00:01:53.074 --> 00:01:55.612
It only succeeds if that block encodes geometry.

00:01:57.342 --> 00:02:01.453
Along the bottom, an ordinary video transformer learns to denoise the clip.

00:02:01.873 --> 00:02:05.893
Along the top, a frozen geometry model turns the clip into features,

00:02:05.893 --> 00:02:09.242
noised and half masked, for a Geometry DiT to denoise.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:13.716
Its only view of the scene is this arrow: the video model's features.

00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:15.899
So its gradient pushes geometry into them.

00:02:18.092 --> 00:02:21.418
In the released code, the teacher is Depth Anything 3.

00:02:21.418 --> 00:02:24.411
Its twelfth layer gives a thousand and twenty-four channels.

00:02:24.831 --> 00:02:28.932
A frozen random projection squeezes that to two hundred and fifty-six.

00:02:29.352 --> 00:02:32.496
Every four frames are averaged: forty-nine become thirteen.

00:02:32.916 --> 00:02:36.116
Then sixteen channels, shaped exactly like a video latent.

00:02:36.536 --> 00:02:39.907
Half its tokens are masked, and the geometry loss is weighted by

00:02:39.907 --> 00:02:41.031
zero point zero three.

00:02:42.842 --> 00:02:43.872
One training step.

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:47.648
The clip goes through the video transformer, forty blocks, and gets

00:02:47.648 --> 00:02:49.021
the usual video loss.

00:02:49.441 --> 00:02:53.270
The frozen teacher's features are the target for the four-block Geometry DiT.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.372
It reads block twenty through cross-attention, and the gradient flows back.

00:02:57.372 --> 00:02:59.046
Nothing flows the other way.

00:02:59.466 --> 00:03:02.422
After training, the teacher and the branch are deleted.

00:03:02.422 --> 00:03:04.064
A plain video model ships.

00:03:05.592 --> 00:03:09.895
The cost: on the released model, the video transformer is sixteen point

00:03:09.895 --> 00:03:11.330
three nine billion parameters.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:16.350
The branch adds one point seven three billion in training, and nothing when generating.

00:03:17.842 --> 00:03:18.452
Chapter three.

00:03:18.452 --> 00:03:19.979
From frames to a gripper.

00:03:24.342 --> 00:03:25.308
Then the extractor.

00:03:25.308 --> 00:03:29.172
A vision-language model and SAM 2 find the object and the gripper,

00:03:29.172 --> 00:03:31.748
and a pose model fits the gripper's shape.

00:03:32.168 --> 00:03:36.429
A point tracker follows the gripper, and finds it again when tracking suddenly drops.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:41.836
Bad poses are repaired, a grasp is chosen, and inverse kinematics makes the motion.

00:03:41.836 --> 00:03:43.617
This part is not released.

00:03:45.342 --> 00:03:45.891
Chapter four.

00:03:45.891 --> 00:03:46.988
Does it hold up?

00:03:52.342 --> 00:03:53.871
TesserAct outputs depth directly.

00:03:53.871 --> 00:03:57.310
Lift its frames to 3D, and the arm smears.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.987
GEM-4D's frames, lifted the same way, hold together.

00:04:02.592 --> 00:04:06.531
On RLBench, where depth is exact, the point-cloud error falls to less

00:04:06.531 --> 00:04:08.172
than half the best baseline's.

00:04:09.592 --> 00:04:14.766
The headline: twenty-two point seven to seventy-five point one percent on seven

00:04:14.766 --> 00:04:18.646
tasks, with TesserAct's videos run through GEM-4D's own extractor.

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:21.231
Before you quote it:

00:04:22.781 --> 00:04:26.047
There is no success number for the same model without the branch.

00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:28.107
No trial counts, and no spread.

00:04:28.953 --> 00:04:33.688
And the real-world sixty-one to eighty-one percent is fifteen people rating videos.

00:04:33.688 --> 00:04:34.871
No robot moved.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:40.550
Real robot runs exist: a generated cup pickup beside the real

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.080
arm, then a pepper.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.992
Demonstrations, without a success rate.

00:04:45.642 --> 00:04:49.515
The thing to keep: geometry can be taught through a branch you delete.

00:04:49.515 --> 00:04:52.792
The generator keeps the structure and pays nothing to run it.

00:04:54.192 --> 00:04:54.942
To recap.

00:04:54.942 --> 00:04:56.816
Pixels alone cannot fix depth.

00:04:56.816 --> 00:04:59.439
A deleted branch teaches the model geometry.

00:04:59.439 --> 00:05:02.813
And the success headline still needs a fair baseline.

00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:05.342
Every source is in the full article.

00:05:05.342 --> 00:05:05.945
I'm Nico.

00:05:05.945 --> 00:05:06.246
Bye!
